<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/19th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Treaty · Peace</p><h1 class="enc-title">Treaty of Córdoba</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1821</span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexican-war-of-independence">Mexican War of Independence</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Treaty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cordoba">Córdoba</a> was signed on 24 August 1821 at Córdoba, Veracruz, by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/agustin-de-iturbide">Agustín de Iturbide</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/juan-odonoju">Juan O'Donojú</a>, the last Spanish head of government of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viceroyalty-of-new-spain">New Spain</a>. It accepted the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plan-of-iguala">Plan of Iguala</a>, recognising <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a> as an independent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constitutional-monarchy">constitutional monarchy</a>, and opened the way for the Army of the Three Guarantees to enter <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico-city">Mexico City</a> in September. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> rejected it, but it marked the end of Spanish rule in New Spain.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/treaty-of-cordoba.jpg" alt="Treaty of Córdoba" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jaontiveros · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tratados_de_C%C3%B3rdoba.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
